{"task_id": "Mbpp/6", "solution": "def differ_At_One_Bit_Pos(num1: int, num2: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if two non-negative integers differ by exactly one bit in their binary representations.\n    Assumes inputs are non-negative as per critical requirements.\n    \"\"\"\n    xor_result = num1 ^ num2\n    # Count the number of set bits (1s) in the XOR result.\n    # A single set bit means the numbers differed at exactly one position.\n    return bin(xor_result).count('1') == 1"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/6", "solution": "```python\ndef differ_At_One_Bit_Pos(num1: int, num2: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if two integers differ at exactly one bit position.\n    This is true if their XOR result is a power of two.\n    \"\"\"\n    xor_result = num1 ^ num2\n    # A positive number x is a power of 2 if (x > 0) and ((x & (x - 1)) == 0).\n    return (xor_result > 0) and ((xor_result & (xor_result - 1)) == 0)\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/2", "solution": "```python\ndef similar_elements(list1, list2):\n    \"\"\"\n    Identifies and returns common elements between two input lists.\n    The order of elements in the returned list is not guaranteed.\n    \"\"\"\n    set_list1 = set(list1)\n    set_list2 = set(list2)\n    common_elements_set = set_list1 & set_list2\n    return list(common_elements_set)\n```"}
