{"task_id": "Mbpp/92", "solution": "```python\ndef is_undulating(number: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a number is undulating, meaning its digits alternate between two distinct digits.\n    Returns False if the number has fewer than three digits or if the first two digits are identical.\n    \"\"\"\n    s_number = str(number)\n\n    if len(s_number) < 3:\n        return False\n\n    if s_number[0] == s_number[1]:\n        return False\n\n    for i in range(2, len(s_number)):\n        if s_number[i] != s_number[i - 2]:\n            return False\n\n    return True\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/92", "solution": "def is_undulating(number: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a given integer is an undulating number.\n    An undulating number has alternating digits (e.g., 1212121).\n    \"\"\"\n    s = str(number)\n    n = len(s)\n\n    # Single-digit numbers are undulating.\n    if n < 2:\n        return True\n\n    # Compare s[i] with s[i+2] for all valid i.\n    for i in range(n - 2): # Loop up to n-3 to allow s[i+2] access.\n        if s[i] != s[i+2]:\n            return False # Not undulating if any pair doesn't match.\n\n    return True # All pairs matched, so it is undulating."}
