{"task_id": "Mbpp/113", "solution": "```python\nclass StringInput:\n    \"\"\"Encapsulates the input string for validation.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, value: str):\n        self.value = value\n\nclass ValidationResult:\n    \"\"\"Encapsulates the boolean outcome of the validation.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, is_integer: bool):\n        self.is_integer = is_integer\n\nclass IntegerValidator:\n    \"\"\"Contains the core logic to validate if a string represents an integer.\"\"\"\n    def is_valid(self, input_obj: StringInput) -> ValidationResult:\n        s_value = input_obj.value\n\n        # BR3: An empty string does not represent an integer.\n        if not s_value:\n            return ValidationResult(False)\n\n        start_index = 0\n        # BR2: A string may optionally start with a single '+' or '-' sign.\n        if s_value[0] == '+' or s_value[0] == '-':\n            start_index = 1\n\n        # BR4: A string containing only a sign ('+' or '-') does not represent an integer.\n        # This ensures there are digits following an optional sign.\n        if start_index == len(s_value):\n            return ValidationResult(False)\n\n        # BR1, BR5: Ensures remaining characters are digits and handles non-digit characters.\n        for char in s_value[start_index:]:\n            if not ('0' <= char <= '9'):\n                return ValidationResult(False)\n\n        # If all checks pass, the string is a valid integer.\n        return ValidationResult(True)\n\ndef check_integer(input_string: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a given string represents an integer based on defined business rules.\n    This function acts as a facade to the IntegerValidator component.\n    \"\"\"\n    string_input_obj = StringInput(input_string)\n    validator = IntegerValidator()\n    validation_result_obj = validator.is_valid(string_input_obj)\n    return validation_result_obj.is_integer\n```"}
