{"task_id": "Mbpp/120", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\ndef max_product_tuple(input_list: list[tuple[float | int, float | int]]) -> float | int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the maximum absolute product from pairs of numbers in a list of tuples.\n    It iterates through each tuple, computes the absolute product, and tracks the maximum found.\n    \"\"\"\n    max_abs_product = float('-inf')\n\n    for num1, num2 in input_list:\n        product = num1 * num2\n        abs_product = abs(product)\n        max_abs_product = max(max_abs_product, abs_product)\n\n    return max_abs_product\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/119", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef search(array: List[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the single unique element in a sorted array where all other elements appear twice.\n    It uses a binary search approach leveraging element parities.\n    \"\"\"\n    low = 0\n    high = len(array) - 1\n\n    while low < high:\n        mid = low + (high - low) // 2\n\n        # If mid is at an even index\n        if mid % 2 == 0:\n            # If array[mid] is the first element of a pair, the unique element must be to its right.\n            if array[mid] == array[mid + 1]:\n                low = mid + 2\n            # Otherwise, array[mid] is either the unique element or the second of a pair, so search left.\n            else:\n                high = mid\n        # If mid is at an odd index\n        else:\n            # If array[mid] is the second element of a pair, the unique element must be to its right.\n            if array[mid] == array[mid - 1]:\n                low = mid + 1\n            # Otherwise, array[mid] is either the unique element or the first of a pair, so search left.\n            else:\n                high = mid\n\n    return array[low]\n```\n"}
