{"task_id": "Mbpp/239", "solution": "```python\ndef _count_sequences(current_length: int, last_element: int, m: int, n: int, memo: dict) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Recursively counts valid sequences starting with last_element and of a given length.\n    Uses memoization to store results of subproblems.\n    \"\"\"\n    if current_length == n:\n        return 1\n\n    if (current_length, last_element) in memo:\n        return memo[(current_length, last_element)]\n\n    count = 0\n    start_next_element = 2 * last_element\n\n    # Iterate for the next element, ensuring it is within the bounds [start_next_element, m].\n    for next_element in range(start_next_element, m + 1):\n        count += _count_sequences(current_length + 1, next_element, m, n, memo)\n\n    memo[(current_length, last_element)] = count\n    return count\n\ndef get_total_number_of_sequences(m: int, n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the total number of sequences of length n, adhering to specific constraints.\n    Each element must be positive, greater than or equal to twice the previous, and less than or equal to m.\n    \"\"\"\n    total_sequences = 0\n    memo = {} # Dictionary for memoization.\n\n    # Iterate through all possible first elements from 1 to m.\n    for first_element in range(1, m + 1):\n        total_sequences += _count_sequences(1, first_element, m, n, memo)\n\n    return total_sequences\n```\n"}
