{"task_id": "Mbpp/296", "solution": "```python\ndef get_Inv_Count(arr: list) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the number of inversions in a given array.\n    An inversion is a pair (i, j) such that i < j and arr[i] > arr[j].\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(arr)\n    if n <= 1:\n        return 0\n\n    def _merge_sort_and_count(current_arr):\n        inversions = 0\n        length = len(current_arr)\n\n        if length <= 1:\n            return current_arr, 0\n\n        mid = length // 2\n        left_half, left_inversions = _merge_sort_and_count(current_arr[:mid])\n        right_half, right_inversions = _merge_sort_and_count(current_arr[mid:])\n\n        inversions += left_inversions + right_inversions\n\n        # Merge step to count split inversions\n        merged_arr = []\n        i, j = 0, 0\n        while i < len(left_half) and j < len(right_half):\n            if left_half[i] <= right_half[j]:\n                merged_arr.append(left_half[i])\n                i += 1\n            else:\n                merged_arr.append(right_half[j])\n                inversions += (len(left_half) - i)\n                j += 1\n\n        merged_arr.extend(left_half[i:])\n        merged_arr.extend(right_half[j:])\n\n        return merged_arr, inversions\n\n    _, total_inversions = _merge_sort_and_count(arr)\n    return total_inversions\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/297", "solution": "```python\ndef flatten_list(nested_list):\n    \"\"\"\n    Flattens a given nested list structure into a single-level list.\n    Recursively expands all sublists while preserving the original order of elements.\n    \"\"\"\n    result = []\n    for item in nested_list:\n        if isinstance(item, list):\n            result.extend(flatten_list(item))\n        else:\n            result.append(item)\n    return result\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/300", "solution": "```python\ndef _factorial(x: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the factorial of a non-negative integer x.\"\"\"\n    if x == 0:\n        return 1\n    res = 1\n    for i in range(1, x + 1):\n        res *= i\n    return res\n\ndef _combinations(n: int, k: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the binomial coefficient C(n, k) using factorials.\"\"\"\n    if k < 0 or k > n:\n        return 0\n    # C(n, k) = n! / (k! * (n-k)!)\n    return _factorial(n) // (_factorial(k) * _factorial(n - k))\n\ndef count_binary_seq(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the count of binary sequences of length 2n where the sum of the first n bits\n    equals the sum of the last n bits.\n    \"\"\"\n    total_count = 0\n    for k in range(n + 1):\n        combinations_nk = _combinations(n, k)\n        total_count += combinations_nk * combinations_nk\n    return total_count\n```\n"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/299", "solution": "```python\ndef max_aggregate(data: list[tuple[str, int]]) -> tuple[str, int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Aggregates scores for names and finds the name with the maximum total score.\n    Returns a tuple of the max name and its total score.\n    \"\"\"\n    aggregated_scores = {}\n    for name, score in data:\n        aggregated_scores[name] = aggregated_scores.get(name, 0) + score\n\n    max_name = None\n    max_score = float('-inf')\n\n    for name, total_score in aggregated_scores.items():\n        if total_score > max_score:\n            max_score = total_score\n            max_name = name\n\n    # The problem assertion implies int output for score.\n    # If the input list is empty, max_score would be float('-inf').\n    # For a non-empty list, it will be an integer.\n    # Adhering to \"Trust the caller's inputs\" and \"simplest logic\",\n    # we return float('-inf') if no scores were aggregated.\n    return (max_name, int(max_score)) if max_name is not None else (None, 0)\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/301", "solution": "```python\ndef dict_depth(input_dict: dict) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the maximum depth of nested dictionaries within the input_dict.\n    An empty dictionary or a dictionary with no nested dictionaries has a depth of 1.\n    \"\"\"\n    max_nested_depth = 0\n    for value in input_dict.values():\n        if isinstance(value, dict):\n            nested_depth = dict_depth(value)\n            max_nested_depth = max(max_nested_depth, nested_depth)\n    return 1 + max_nested_depth\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/305", "solution": "```python\ndef start_withp(input_list: list[str]) -> tuple[str, str]:\n    \"\"\"Extracts the first two words starting with 'P' from a list of strings.\"\"\"\n    collected_words = []\n    for s in input_list:\n        words = s.split()\n        for word in words:\n            if word.startswith('P'):\n                collected_words.append(word)\n                if len(collected_words) == 2:\n                    break\n        if len(collected_words) == 2:\n            break\n    return tuple(collected_words)\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/300", "solution": "def _combinations(N: int, K: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the binomial coefficient C(N, K).\n    Assumes 0 <= K <= N based on problem context.\"\"\"\n    res = 1\n    for i in range(K):\n        res = res * (N - i) // (i + 1)\n    return res\n\ndef count_binary_seq(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the count of binary sequences for length 2n.\n    The sum of the first n bits equals the sum of the last n bits.\"\"\"\n    return _combinations(2 * n, n)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/306", "solution": "```python\ndef max_sum_increasing_subseq(array: list[int], length: int, i: int, k: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the maximum sum of an increasing subsequence from a prefix up to index 'i',\n    mandatorily including the element at index 'k' (where k > i).\n    \"\"\"\n\n    # dp_prefix_sums[idx] stores the maximum sum of an increasing subsequence\n    # ending at array[idx] within the prefix 0 to i.\n    dp_prefix_sums = [0] * (i + 1)\n\n    # Populate dp_prefix_sums for elements from 0 to i\n    for idx in range(i + 1):\n        dp_prefix_sums[idx] = array[idx]  # Initialize with the element itself\n        for prev_idx in range(idx):\n            if array[idx] > array[prev_idx]:\n                dp_prefix_sums[idx] = max(dp_prefix_sums[idx], array[idx] + dp_prefix_sums[prev_idx])\n\n    # Calculate the maximum sum including array[k]\n    # Initialize with array[k] itself, as it can be a subsequence of one element.\n    max_sum_ending_at_k = array[k]\n\n    # Iterate through the computed dp_prefix_sums to find if array[k] can extend any subsequence\n    for idx in range(i + 1):\n        if array[k] > array[idx]:\n            max_sum_ending_at_k = max(max_sum_ending_at_k, array[k] + dp_prefix_sums[idx])\n\n    return max_sum_ending_at_k\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/306", "solution": "```python\ndef max_sum_increasing_subseq(arr: list[int], n: int, i: int, k: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the maximum sum of an increasing subsequence within a prefix of `arr`\n    that includes `arr[k]` and is strictly increasing.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Initialize dp array of size n.\n    dp = [0] * n\n\n    # Initialize dp array for prefix up to i.\n    for x in range(i + 1):\n        dp[x] = arr[x]\n\n    # Populate dp array for prefix up to i.\n    for x in range(i + 1):\n        for j in range(x):\n            if arr[x] > arr[j]:\n                dp[x] = max(dp[x], dp[j] + arr[x])\n\n    # Initialize max_prefix_subsequence_sum.\n    max_prefix_subsequence_sum = 0\n\n    # Find the maximum sum from dp array that can precede arr[k].\n    for j in range(i + 1):\n        if arr[j] < arr[k]:\n            max_prefix_subsequence_sum = max(max_prefix_subsequence_sum, dp[j])\n\n    # Add arr[k] to the found max prefix sum and return.\n    return max_prefix_subsequence_sum + arr[k]\n```\n"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/308", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List, Union\n\ndef large_product(list1: List[Union[int, float]], list2: List[Union[int, float]], k: int) -> List[Union[int, float]]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Computes all possible products from two input lists, identifies the k largest,\n    and returns them in descending order.\n    \"\"\"\n    all_products = []\n    for num1 in list1:\n        for num2 in list2:\n            all_products.append(num1 * num2)\n\n    all_products.sort(reverse=True)\n    return all_products[:k]\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/308", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List, Union\n\ndef large_product(list1: List[Union[int, float]], list2: List[Union[int, float]], k: int) -> List[Union[int, float]]:\n    \"\"\"Finds the k largest products from two lists, selecting one factor from each.\n    Returns the products in descending order.\n    \"\"\"\n    all_products = []\n    # Calculate all possible products by selecting one factor from each list.\n    for num1 in list1:\n        for num2 in list2:\n            all_products.append(num1 * num2)\n\n    # Sort all products in descending order to easily pick the largest k.\n    all_products.sort(reverse=True)\n\n    # Return the top k products.\n    return all_products[:k]\n```"}
