{"task_id": "Mbpp/564", "solution": "```python\ndef count_Pairs(numbers, list_length):\n    \"\"\"\n    Counts unordered pairs with unequal elements in a list.\n    Applies the formula: total_pairs - equal_element_pairs.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(numbers)\n\n    # Handle edge case: If the length of the list is less than 2, no pairs can be formed.\n    if n < 2:\n        return 0\n\n    # Calculate total possible unordered pairs from 'n' elements.\n    total_possible_pairs = n * (n - 1) // 2\n\n    # Use a frequency map (dictionary) to count occurrences of each number.\n    frequency_map = {}\n    for num in numbers:\n        frequency_map[num] = frequency_map.get(num, 0) + 1\n\n    # Calculate the number of unordered pairs with equal elements.\n    equal_pairs_count = 0\n    for freq in frequency_map.values():\n        # Only frequencies of 2 or more can form equal pairs\n        if freq >= 2:\n            equal_pairs_count += freq * (freq - 1) // 2\n\n    # Subtract the 'equal_pairs_count' from 'total_possible_pairs' to get the final result.\n    return total_possible_pairs - equal_pairs_count\n```"}
