{"task_id": "Mbpp/580", "solution": "```python\ndef extract_even(nested_tuple: tuple) -> tuple:\n    \"\"\"Recursively processes a nested tuple to remove all odd integers.\n    Preserves original nesting structure with only even numbers.\n    \"\"\"\n    result_list = []\n    for element in nested_tuple:\n        try:\n            # Attempt integer parity check; this will raise TypeError for non-integers.\n            if element % 2 == 0:\n                result_list.append(element)\n        except TypeError:\n            # If not an integer, assume it's a tuple for recursive processing.\n            sub_tuple_result = extract_even(element)\n            if sub_tuple_result:  # Only add non-empty sub-tuples to preserve structure.\n                result_list.append(sub_tuple_result)\n        except Exception:\n            # Ignore elements that are neither integers nor tuples.\n            pass\n    return tuple(result_list)\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/581", "solution": "import math\n\ndef surface_Area(base_edge: float, height: float) -> float:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the total surface area of a square pyramid.\n    It uses the standard geometric formula involving base edge and perpendicular height.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Calculate the slant height (l) of the pyramid.\n    half_base_edge = base_edge / 2\n    slant_height = math.sqrt(height**2 + half_base_edge**2)\n\n    # Calculate the area of the square base.\n    base_area = base_edge**2\n\n    # Calculate the lateral surface area (sum of 4 triangular faces).\n    lateral_area = 2 * base_edge * slant_height\n\n    # The total surface area is the sum of the base area and the lateral area.\n    total_surface_area = base_area + lateral_area\n    return total_surface_area"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/589", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\ndef perfect_squares(num1: int, num2: int) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates and returns a list of all perfect squares within the inclusive range [num1, num2].\n    The list is empty if no perfect squares are found in the specified range.\n    \"\"\"\n    perfect_squares_list = []\n\n    start_sqrt = math.ceil(math.sqrt(num1))\n    end_sqrt = math.floor(math.sqrt(num2))\n\n    for i in range(int(start_sqrt), int(end_sqrt) + 1):\n        square = i * i\n        perfect_squares_list.append(square)\n\n    return perfect_squares_list\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/589", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\ndef perfect_squares(num1: int, num2: int) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Identifies all perfect squares within the given inclusive integer range.\n\n    Args:\n        num1: The lower bound of the range (inclusive).\n        num2: The upper bound of the range (inclusive).\n    Returns:\n        A list of perfect squares found within the range, in ascending order.\n    \"\"\"\n    if num1 > num2:\n        return []\n\n    result = []\n    # Start checking from the smallest integer whose square is >= num1\n    start_val = max(1, math.ceil(math.sqrt(num1))) if num1 > 0 else 1\n\n    # End checking at the largest integer whose square is <= num2\n    end_val = math.floor(math.sqrt(num2)) if num2 >= 1 else 0\n\n    for i in range(int(start_val), int(end_val) + 1):\n        square = i * i\n        if num1 <= square <= num2:\n            result.append(square)\n    return result\n```"}
