{"task_id": "Mbpp/592", "solution": "```python\nimport math\n\ndef sum_Of_product(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the sum of products of consecutive binomial coefficients.\n    Sums C(n, k)*C(n, k+1) for k from 0 to n-1 using math.comb.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n    if n == 1:\n        # C(1,0)*C(1,1) = 1*1 = 1\n        return math.comb(1, 0) * math.comb(1, 1)\n\n    total_sum = 0\n    for k in range(n):\n        c_nk = math.comb(n, k)\n        c_nk_plus_1 = math.comb(n, k + 1)\n        total_sum += c_nk * c_nk_plus_1\n\n    return total_sum\n```\n"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/585", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List, Dict, Union\n\ndef expensive_items(dataset: List[Dict], n: int) -> List[Dict]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds and returns the n most expensive items from a dataset.\n    This function handles invalid 'n', empty datasets, and malformed price data.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    class Item:\n        \"\"\"Represents a single item with a name and a price.\"\"\"\n        def __init__(self, name: str, price: Union[float, int, str, None]):\n            self.name = name\n            self.price: Union[float, None] = None\n            try:\n                if price is not None:\n                    self.price = float(price)\n            except (ValueError, TypeError):\n                # Price remains None if conversion fails.\n                pass\n\n        def __lt__(self, other: 'Item') -> bool:\n            \"\"\"Enables sorting Item objects, treating None prices as smallest.\"\"\"\n            if self.price is None:\n                return other.price is not None\n            if other.price is None:\n                return False\n            return self.price < other.price\n\n        def to_dict(self) -> Dict:\n            \"\"\"Converts the Item object back to a dictionary.\"\"\"\n            return {'name': self.name, 'price': self.price}\n\n    class Dataset:\n        \"\"\"Encapsulates a collection of items, performing initial parsing and validation.\"\"\"\n        def __init__(self, raw_items: List[Dict]):\n            self.items: List[Item] = []\n            for raw_item in raw_items:\n                item_name = raw_item.get('name')\n                item_price = raw_item.get('price')\n                if item_name is not None:  # An item must have a name to be processed.\n                    self.items.append(Item(str(item_name), item_price))\n\n        def get_valid_items(self) -> List[Item]:\n            \"\"\"Returns a list of Item objects that have valid (non-None) price values.\"\"\"\n            return [item for item in self.items if item.price is not None]\n\n    # If n is negative or zero, an empty list is returned as per design.\n    if not isinstance(n, int) or n <= 0:\n        return []\n\n    # Initialize Dataset object from raw input to process and validate items.\n    dataset_obj = Dataset(dataset)\n\n    # Retrieve only items with valid (non-None) price values.\n    valid_items = dataset_obj.get_valid_items()\n\n    # If no valid items are found, return an empty list.\n    if not valid_items:\n        return []\n\n    # Sort the valid items by price in descending order.\n    # Items with larger prices will appear first.\n    valid_items.sort(key=lambda item: item.price, reverse=True)\n\n    # Return all valid items if n is greater than or equal to their total count.\n    if n >= len(valid_items):\n        return [item.to_dict() for item in valid_items]\n    # Otherwise, return the top n most expensive items.\n    else:\n        return [item.to_dict() for item in valid_items[:n]]\n\n```"}
