{"task_id": "Mbpp/598", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List, Tuple\n\ndef _get_digits_and_count(number: int) -> Tuple[List[int], int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Extracts digits from an integer and counts them.\n    Converts the number to its string representation for processing.\n    \"\"\"\n    number_str = str(number)\n    num_digits = len(number_str)\n    digits_list = [int(digit_char) for digit_char in number_str]\n    return digits_list, num_digits\n\ndef armstrong_number(number: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Determines if a given integer is an Armstrong number.\n    Returns True if the number equals the sum of its digits each raised to the power of the total number of digits.\n    \"\"\"\n    if number < 0:\n        return False\n\n    # Single-digit numbers are trivially Armstrong numbers (e.g., 5 is 5^1).\n    if 0 <= number <= 9:\n        return True\n\n    digits_list, num_digits = _get_digits_and_count(number)\n\n    sum_of_powers = 0\n    for digit in digits_list:\n        sum_of_powers += digit ** num_digits\n\n    return sum_of_powers == number\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/597", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import Any\n\ndef find_kth(array1: list[Any], array2: list[Any], k: int) -> Any:\n    \"\"\"Finds the kth smallest element from two sorted arrays.\n    It uses a two-pointer approach to efficiently locate the element in O(k) time.\n    \"\"\"\n    p1 = 0\n    p2 = 0\n    m = len(array1)\n    n = len(array2)\n\n    # Advance pointers k-1 times to effectively \"discard\" the first k-1 smallest elements.\n    for _ in range(k - 1):\n        if p1 < m and p2 < n:\n            if array1[p1] < array2[p2]:\n                p1 += 1\n            else:\n                p2 += 1\n        elif p1 < m:  # array2 is exhausted\n            p1 += 1\n        elif p2 < n:  # array1 is exhausted\n            p2 += 1\n        # The problem statement guarantees k is valid, so arrays won't be simultaneously exhausted prematurely.\n\n    # After k-1 advancements, the next smallest element is the kth element.\n    if p1 < m and p2 < n:\n        return min(array1[p1], array2[p2])\n    elif p1 < m:  # array2 is exhausted, take from array1\n        return array1[p1]\n    else:  # array1 is exhausted, take from array2 (p2 < n must be true)\n        return array2[p2]\n```\n"}
