{"task_id": "Mbpp/620", "solution": "```python\nfrom typing import List\n\ndef largest_subset(numbers: List[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the size of the largest subset from a list of numbers\n    where every pair within that subset is divisible.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(numbers)\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n\n    # Sort numbers in ascending order to simplify divisibility checks.\n    numbers.sort()\n\n    # dp[i] stores the length of the largest divisible subset ending with numbers[i].\n    dp = [1] * n\n\n    # Iterate through numbers to fill the dp array.\n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(i):\n            # If numbers[i] is divisible by numbers[j], update dp[i].\n            if numbers[i] % numbers[j] == 0:\n                dp[i] = max(dp[i], dp[j] + 1)\n\n    # The maximum value in dp array is the size of the largest divisible subset.\n    return max(dp)\n```\n"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/619", "solution": "```python\ndef move_num(input_string: str) -> str:\n    \"\"\"\n    Rearranges characters in a string, moving all numeric characters to the end\n    while preserving the relative order of both non-numeric and numeric characters.\n    \"\"\"\n    non_numeric_chars = []\n    numeric_chars = []\n\n    for char in input_string:\n        if char.isdigit():\n            numeric_chars.append(char)\n        else:\n            non_numeric_chars.append(char)\n\n    return \"\".join(non_numeric_chars) + \"\".join(numeric_chars)\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/620", "solution": "```python\ndef largest_subset(numbers: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the size of the largest divisible subset from a list of numbers.\n    Assumes input 'numbers' contains only positive integers as per critical requirements.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not numbers:\n        return 0\n\n    # Sort the list of numbers in ascending order for dynamic programming.\n    numbers.sort()\n\n    n = len(numbers)\n    # If the list has only one element, the largest subset size is 1.\n    if n == 1:\n        return 1\n\n    # dp[i] stores the size of the largest divisible subset ending with numbers[i].\n    dp = [1] * n\n\n    for i in range(n):\n        for j in range(i):\n            if numbers[i] % numbers[j] == 0:\n                dp[i] = max(dp[i], dp[j] + 1)\n\n    # The maximum value in the dp array represents the largest subset size found.\n    return max(dp)\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/622", "solution": "def get_median(list1: list[float], list2: list[float], size: int) -> float:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the median of two sorted lists of the same size.\n    It finds the (size-1)th and (size)th elements of the conceptually merged list.\n    \"\"\"\n    i = 0  # Pointer for list1\n    j = 0  # Pointer for list2\n    median_elem1 = 0.0 # Stores the element at combined index (size - 1)\n    median_elem2 = 0.0 # Stores the element at combined index (size)\n\n    # Iterate 'size + 1' times to find the elements at combined indices (size - 1) and size.\n    for count in range(size + 1):\n        if i == size:  # list1 is exhausted\n            current_element = list2[j]\n            j += 1\n        elif j == size:  # list2 is exhausted\n            current_element = list1[i]\n            i += 1\n        elif list1[i] <= list2[j]:\n            current_element = list1[i]\n            i += 1\n        else:\n            current_element = list2[j]\n            j += 1\n\n        if count == size - 1:\n            median_elem1 = current_element\n        if count == size:\n            median_elem2 = current_element\n\n    return (median_elem1 + median_elem2) / 2.0"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/622", "solution": "```python\ndef get_median(list1, list2, n):\n    \"\"\"Calculates the median of combined elements from two equally sized, sorted lists.\n    It finds the n-th and (n+1)-th elements of the merged sequence to compute the median.\n    \"\"\"\n    i = 0\n    j = 0\n    m1 = -1\n    m2 = -1\n\n    # Iterate n + 1 times to find the n-th and (n+1)-th elements\n    for _ in range(n + 1):\n        m1 = m2  # m1 stores the previous median candidate\n\n        # Compare elements from both lists, handling cases where one list is exhausted\n        if i < n and (j == n or list1[i] <= list2[j]):\n            m2 = list1[i]\n            i += 1\n        else:\n            m2 = list2[j]\n            j += 1\n\n    # The median is the average of the n-th (m1) and (n+1)-th (m2) elements.\n    return (m1 + m2) / 2.0\n```"}
