{"task_id": "Mbpp/785", "solution": "```python\ndef tuple_str_int(input_string: str) -> tuple[int, ...]:\n    \"\"\"Converts a string representation of an integer tuple into an actual tuple of integers.\n    It parses strings formatted like \"(7, 8, 9)\" into (7, 8, 9).\"\"\"\n    # Remove leading/trailing parentheses and then split the string by comma.\n    # Assumes valid input string like \"(N1, N2, N3)\".\n    content_string = input_string[1:-1]\n\n    # Handle the case of an empty tuple string \"()\"\n    if not content_string:\n        return ()\n\n    # Split the string into individual number strings and convert each to an integer.\n    # Each string element is stripped of whitespace before conversion.\n    str_numbers = content_string.split(',')\n    int_numbers = [int(s.strip()) for s in str_numbers]\n\n    # Convert the list of integers into a tuple and return it.\n    return tuple(int_numbers)\n```"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/786", "solution": "```python\ndef right_insertion(sorted_list: list, value: any) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Locates the rightmost insertion point for a value in a sorted list.\n    The insertion point ensures the list remains sorted and is to the right of equal elements.\"\"\"\n    low = 0\n    high = len(sorted_list)\n\n    while low < high:\n        mid = (low + high) // 2\n        if sorted_list[mid] <= value:\n            low = mid + 1\n        else:\n            high = mid\n    return low\n```"}
